      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "fd194830-9a6f-5a3b-91e5-5a310c786578",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jean-Philippe Rameau, Voltaire, Charles Dill, Giacomo Puccini, Henri Larrivée, Cesira Ferrani\n- Works: Castor et Pollux, Samson, Manon Lescaut, La bohème\n- Roles: high priest, Mimì, title role\n- Topics: opera composition, revivals, world premieres, operatic debuts\n\nFacts:\n- Charles Dill proposes that Jean-Philippe Rameau composed Castor et Pollux in 1737 just after working with Voltaire on the unfinished opera Samson.\n- Charles Dill proposes that after Samson was left incomplete, Jean-Philippe Rameau composed Castor et Pollux using Voltaire's aesthetics.\n- In 1755, Henri Larrivée made his first solo appearance as a high priest in a revival of Jean-Philippe Rameau's Castor et Pollux.\n- Giacomo Puccini composed the four-act opera Manon Lescaut between 1890 and 1893.\n- Cesira Ferrani, born on May 8, 1863, was an Italian operatic soprano.\n- Cesira Ferrani created the title role in Giacomo Puccini's Manon Lescaut at its 1893 world premiere.\n- In 1896, Cesira Ferrani debuted Mimì in the original production of Giacomo Puccini's La bohème.\n- Cesira Ferrani died on May 4, 1943, in Pollone.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2e30900a-1614-5227-aed3-dae446bddb01",
        "text": "Page: Castor et Pollux. Sentence: Charles Dill proposes that Rameau had composed the 1737 opera just after working with Voltaire on the opera \"Samson\" that was never completed, after which he composed \"Castor et Pollux\" implementing Voltaire's aesthetics.\nPage: Manon Lescaut (Puccini). Sentence: Manon Lescaut is an opera in four acts by Giacomo Puccini, composed between 1890 and 1893.\nPage: Henri Larrivée. Sentence: He made his first solo appearance as a high priest in a 1755 revival of Rameau's \"Castor et Pollux\".\nPage: Cesira Ferrani. Sentence: Cesira Ferrani (May 8, 1863 in Turin – May 4, 1943 in Pollone) was an Italian operatic soprano who is best known for debuting two of the most iconic roles in opera history, Mimì in the original 1896 production of Giacomo Puccini's \"La bohème\" and the title role in Puccini's \"Manon Lescaut\" in its 1893 world premiere.",
